 {
  "_description": "# Help and flag placement\n\nWhere flags may go, which commands differ, help levels and short names.\n",
- "placement": "# A flag goes after its level\n\nA command has levels: the program, its subcommand, the action (`aos-agent`, `ctl`, `stop`). A flag belongs to one level and may go anywhere after that level's name, also after later names and arguments. These are the same:\n\n```sh\naos-agent -d /srv/d ctl stop bob\naos-agent ctl stop bob -d /srv/d\naos-agent ctl stop -t bob\naos-tools add tools/clock --name now\n```\n\nThis holds for every aos command, the parts in `aos/cli/parts/` too (`aos-json get FILE#/a --str`, `aos-eca-turn /abs/def --one`). Exceptions, where words are handed on as they are: everything after the folder in `aos-exec FOLDER ARGS` goes to that folder's program; `aos-sandbox CONFIG PROG ...` hands PROG and its args on; `aos-tree-start [--force] ARGS` hands ARGS to aos-exec (`--force` goes first); `aos-mcp-launch SERVER_FILE` takes no flags; `aos-json set`, `append` and `merge` take their flags before FILE, because the value may be any word (`aos-json set --str FILE#/a -x`). A word after an argument that looks like a negative number (`-3`) or holds a space stays an argument.\n",
+ "placement": "# A flag goes after its level\n\nA command has levels: the program, its subcommand, the action (`aos-agent`, `ctl`, `stop`). A flag belongs to one level and may go anywhere after that level's name, also after later names and arguments. These are the same:\n\n```sh\naos-agent -d /srv/d ctl stop bob\naos-agent ctl stop bob -d /srv/d\naos-agent ctl stop -t bob\naos-tools add tools/clock --name now\n```\n\nThis holds for every aos command, the parts in `aos/cli/parts/` too (`aos-json get FILE#/a --str`, `aos-eca-turn /abs/def --one`). Exceptions, where words are handed on as they are: everything after the folder in `aos-exec FOLDER ARGS` goes to that folder's program; `aos-sandbox CONFIG PROG ...` hands PROG and its args on;`aos-mcp-launch SERVER_FILE` takes no flags; `aos-json set`, `append` and `merge` take their flags before FILE, because the value may be any word (`aos-json set --str FILE#/a -x`). A word after an argument that looks like a negative number (`-3`) or holds a space stays an argument.\n",
  "target": "# Naming the agent in aos-agent\n\n`aos-agent ctl` start / stop / sleep / wake / pause / resume / where take the agent as `-t NAME` or as the last argument. say, listen, chat, open, close, watch, tools, llm, system, history, hook, goal and `ctl state` take only `-t` (state's other words are part names). `ctl add / rm / set-def / ls / who` take no `-t`. Two different names = `ArgumentsBad`.\n",
  "text": "# Text ends flag reading\n\nText that is handed on as it is ends flag reading: everything from the first word of `aos-agent say WORDS`, `history summarize RANGE TEXT`, `history set LINE JSON`, `goal set TEXT`, `aos-mailbox send WHAT`, `aos-clock-register add NAME TEXT` is text, so put flags before it. After `--` nothing is a flag: `aos-daemon-ctl add tools/job -- --verbose`, `aos-agent say -t bob -- -hello`.\n",
  "help": "# Help levels\n\nRun `aos-agent --help`, then `aos-agent tools --help`, then `aos-agent tools add --help`. Each prints only its own level. Help needs no live daemon or target. Commands with subcommands work the same: `aos-post send --help`, `aos-json set --help`.\n\nA flag nobody in the command knows gives `FlagUnknown`. No subcommand gives `ArgumentsBad` (`aos-post: error: ArgumentsBad missing subcommand`); an unknown subcommand gives `CommandUnknown`.\n",
diff --git a/proto8/knowledge/aos/kits.json b/proto8/knowledge/aos/kits.json
