 {
-  "_description": "# Kits: ready-made system text and tools for one agent role\n\nA kit is a system text plus a tool set for one role (gatherer, reviewer), laid over a new agent-fsm definition. Kits live in the templates folder, in `templates/kits/` next to `agent-fsm/`; `kits.json` there lists them. A kit is not a template of its own: you make an agent-fsm definition and copy the kit's files over it.\n\n- How to make an agent from a kit: aos/kits.json#/use.\n- How a kit's system text is built from layers: aos/kits.json#/layers.\n- Why a kit cuts tools, and what it leaves out: aos/kits.json#/tools.\n- What a kit does not enforce yet: aos/kits.json#/limits.\n",
-  "use": "# Make an agent from a kit\n\n1. agent_def {\"op\": \"new\", \"template\": \"agent-fsm\", \"path\": \"defs/gather\"}\n2. Copy `templates/kits/gather/system.txt` and `templates/kits/gather/tools.json` over `defs/gather/system.txt` and `defs/gather/tools.json` (tools.json names its packs with {\"$env\": \"AOS_HOME\"}, so it works wherever it is copied).\n3. Set the brain in `defs/gather/llm.json` (aos/brain.json).\n4. agent_def {\"op\": \"check\", \"path\": \"defs/gather\"}: an empty problems list = ready.\n5. Start it with subagent add, and give it a brief: the one task, the paths it may read and write, what to deliver in out/, the finish condition (it copies that into its goal), and the knowledge folder path.\n\nFor a reviewer, take `system.txt` from `templates/kits/review/`; the tools are the same as gather. Several agents of one role can share one definition.\n",
+  "_description": "# Kits: ready-made system text and tools for one agent role\n\nA kit is a system text plus a tool set for one role (gatherer, reviewer), laid over a new agent-fsm definition. Kits live in the templates folder, in `templates/kits/` next to `agent-fsm/`; `kits.json` there lists them. A kit is not a template of its own: it is a folder with a system.txt and a tools.json. You make an agent-fsm definition and copy the kit's files over it (a person can instead give the folder to `aos-agent ctl add --kit`).\n\n- How to make an agent from a kit: aos/kits.json#/use.\n- How a kit's system text is built from layers: aos/kits.json#/layers.\n- Why a kit cuts tools, and what it leaves out: aos/kits.json#/tools.\n- What a kit does not enforce yet: aos/kits.json#/limits.\n",
+  "use": "# Make an agent from a kit\n\n1. agent_def {\"op\": \"new\", \"template\": \"agent-fsm\", \"path\": \"defs/gather\"}\n2. Copy `templates/kits/gather/system.txt` and `templates/kits/gather/tools.json` over `defs/gather/system.txt` and `defs/gather/tools.json` (tools.json names its packs with {\"$env\": \"AOS_HOME\"}, so it works wherever it is copied).\n3. Set the brain in `defs/gather/llm.json` (aos/brain.json).\n4. agent_def {\"op\": \"check\", \"path\": \"defs/gather\"}: an empty problems list = ready.\n5. Start it with subagent add, and give it a brief: the one task, the paths it may read and write, what to deliver in out/, the finish condition (it copies that into its goal), and the knowledge folder path.\n\nFor a reviewer, do the same with `templates/kits/review/` (its tools.json is the same as gather's). Several agents of one role can share one definition.\n\nA person attaching an agent from a shell does not copy: `aos-agent ctl add --kit KIT-FOLDER ...` (or `ctl set-def --kit KIT-FOLDER NAME DEF`) writes the kit's system.txt and tools.json into that agent's stack as its own copies, and leaves the definition as it is. Only agent-fsm definitions take a kit (an eca one is refused). `aos-agent system reset` and `aos-agent tools reset` drop the kit again.\n",
   "layers": "# Layers of a kit's system text\n\nA system text has no include and no variables: it is sent whole on every brain call, so it is assembled once, when the definition is made, top to bottom:\n\n1. general: `kits/general.A.txt` (tier A = strong general models): start (self_state, read the knowledge base, goal set), workspace folder (notes.md, out/, trash/), work, report (one mailbox_send to the lead), stuck, notice board, stop.\n2. site (optional, not shipped): your own rules (who to report to, language, local don'ts, where the notice board is). Put it between general and role.\n3. role: `kits/roles/gather.txt` or `kits/roles/review.txt`.\n4. task: the brief, sent in the letter that starts the agent; not in system.txt.\n\ngeneral.A.txt has placeholders: `<KNOWLEDGE>` (path of the knowledge folder), `<BOARD>` (how to read a notice board: a command, or a file to read; drop the line if there is none), `<OWN_BOARD>` (how the agent posts who it waits for, or drop the line). The shipped `gather/system.txt` and `review/system.txt` say the brief names the knowledge folder, resolve BOARD to board_read (who = the one waited on, or \"user\" for the user's notice) and OWN_BOARD to board_wait (who and what when it stops to wait, done = true when the answer comes), and stay within 3,000 characters. If you change a layer, assemble again in the same order.\n",
   "tools": "# Why a kit cuts tools\n\nEvery tool's definition is sent on every brain call, so the tool list is the biggest fixed cost, larger than the system text. Measured 2026-11-14 as sent: agent-fsm as shipped has 40 tools, about 50,500 characters; the gather kit had 24 tools, about 30,100 characters; board_read and board_wait were added after that (26 tools).\n\nThe gather tool set keeps look, change (only write, edit, mkdir), gadgets, todo, mailbox, comm (only board_read and board_wait; board_set, board_clear and board_ls are turned off), goal, json (read only), index and self_state. It leaves out shell, delete, events, subagent, def, jsonx and kernel_gate. self_tools, self_system, self_llm, self_history and self_ctl are turned off, so the agent cannot give itself back a tool or rewrite its own rules. How a single tool is turned off: aos/packs.json.\n",
   "limits": "# What a kit does not enforce yet\n\n- Writing only in the workspace is a rule in the system text; file tools themselves do not stop a path outside it, and no hook enforces it yet.\n- Only a tier A general text and two roles (gatherer, reviewer) exist. A small model may skip parts of the start and stop rules (notes.md, the handoff).\n"
